\section{The residual-regression form (\code{cov = FALSE})}{

This is the model brms 2.23.0 fits for \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and \code{arma()}
when \code{cov} is not set. Within each group, in time order, let
\eqn{r_t = y_t - \mu_t} and
\deqn{e_t = r_t - \sum_{i=1}^{q} \theta_i e_{t-i},}
with \eqn{e_s = 0} before the group's first row. The mean of row
\eqn{t} becomes
\deqn{\mu^*_t = \mu_t + \sum_{i=1}^{q} \theta_i e_{t-i} +
      \sum_{i=1}^{p} \phi_i e_{t-i},}
and every row keeps the family's own density at \eqn{\mu^*_t}. The
MA part enters \eqn{e} and the AR part does not, exactly as in
brms's Stan code. So:
\itemize{
\item It is a CONDITIONAL likelihood. A group starts with no
residuals behind it, so lag \eqn{i} first reaches the row at
within-group position \eqn{i + 1}: the FIRST row of a group gets
no lagged term, a row at position \eqn{k} gets only the lags up
to \eqn{k - 1}, and from position \eqn{\max(p, q) + 1}{max(p,
    q) + 1} onward a row gets all of them. None of the opening rows
gets the stationary distribution the covariance form gives them.
For one series this is the conditional sum of squares of
\code{stats::arima(method = "CSS")}.
\item The coefficients \code{ar[i]} and \code{ma[i]} are unconstrained reals,
as in brms, because nothing in this likelihood needs a stationary
or invertible process. A prior or a bound with \code{set_prior(class = "ar")} acts on them directly, at any order.
\item \code{sigma} is the sd of each row around \eqn{\mu^*_t}, which for
a correct model is the innovation sd.
\item The lag is counted in ROWS of the group, sorted by \code{time}, as
brms counts it: a group that skips a time point treats the next
row as one step later. The covariance form counts levels instead
(see below).
\item Families: \code{gaussian()} and \code{student()}, the two families brms
gives a residual \code{y - mu}. A predicted \code{nu ~ ...} works. Every other
family has the model of "Latent residuals" instead. With a
non-identity link the term is added to \code{mu} on the response
scale, after the inverse link, as brms's Stan code adds it.
\item It combines with \code{weights()}, \code{cens()}, \code{trunc()}, \code{mi()} on
the response (the residual is then taken against the imputed
value), random effects and \code{rescor = TRUE}, as in brms. \code{se()} is
refused, as brms refuses it.
\item After the fit, \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} and
\code{predict(type = "response")} give brms's one-step mean
\eqn{\mu^*_t}, which reads the OBSERVED earlier residuals; so
\code{residuals()} are against it. \code{newdata} must then carry the
response, and its rows form their own groups in their own time
order. \code{\link[=predict.frmtmb_fit]{predict.frmtmb_fit()}} draws each row around its one-step
mean, as brms's \code{posterior_predict()} does. \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} instead
runs the recursion over its own draws, so a replicate carries the
fitted serial dependence; on \code{newdata} it starts each group from
an empty past and needs no response, which makes it the
forecasting route. \code{conditional_effects()} and \code{emmeans()} drop
the term, as brms's do. \code{\link[=autocor_matrix]{autocor_matrix()}} and
\code{residuals(type = "osa")} are refused: this form defines no
correlation matrix, and its tape reads the response as data.
The pointwise log-density of this form is each row's own
density given its group's observed past, which is what brms's
\code{log_lik()} returns. \code{arma_cond_resp()} and \code{arma_cond_dpars()}
(\link{frmtmb-sampling-api}) are what a sampling extension reads to
build it.
}
}

\section{The pointwise log-density}{

A residual covariance term (the covariance form of \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and
\code{arma()}, \code{cosy()}, \code{unstr()}, \code{fcor()} and the terms of
\code{\link[=frmtmb_register_autocor]{frmtmb_register_autocor()}}) has no density per row. brms's
\code{log_lik()} gives the leave-one-out conditional density instead, the
density of one row given the other rows of its group, after the loo
package's vignette on non-factorized models, and
\code{frmtmb.sample::log_lik()} gives the same, through
\code{autocor_row_loglik()} (\link{frmtmb-sampling-api}). With \eqn{Q} the
precision matrix of a group's response and \eqn{e} its residual, row
\eqn{i} is normal with mean \eqn{y_i - (Qe)_i / Q_{ii}} and variance
\eqn{1 / Q_{ii}}, or for \code{student()} a t with \eqn{\nu + k - 1}
degrees of freedom, \eqn{k} the size of the group. The values are
brms's, so the row sum is NOT the log-likelihood of the fit, and
\code{loo()} on them leaves out one row with the rest of its group kept.
A group missing a time point gives the conditional of the model this
page describes (lags from the time levels), which differs from
brms's for \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and \code{arma()} (lags by position) exactly as
the two likelihoods differ.
}

\section{A fixed residual matrix, \code{fcor(M)}}{

\code{fcor(M)} makes the residuals of all the rows one draw with
covariance \eqn{\sigma^2 M}, or \eqn{D M D} with
\eqn{D = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma_i)} under a predicted \code{sigma ~ ...}, as
brms 2.23.0 fits it. \code{student()} gets the multivariate-t analog, with
a constant \code{nu}. \code{M} is a known matrix in \code{data2} over every row of
the data, and the rows the model drops (missing values) are dropped
from it, as brms drops them. \code{M} may be a correlation or a
covariance; for a covariance, \code{sigma} is a scale factor, as in brms.
A vector is the diagonal. \code{M} must be symmetric and positive
definite. brms 2.23.0 checks the second with
\code{min(eigen(M)$values <= 0)}, which refuses only a matrix whose
eigenvalues are all non-positive; frmtmb refuses every matrix that is
not positive definite. \code{se()} is refused beside it, as brms refuses it
("Invalid addition arguments for this model"). \code{mi()} on the response
works, as in brms: each missing value is a latent coordinate of the
joint draw, integrated out under the Laplace approximation, which on
\code{gaussian()} is exact and gives the fit over the observed rows with
\code{M} cut to them. On \code{student()} the Laplace error is a function of
\code{nu} and the numbers of rows and of missing values alone, and the
objective takes it off, so the fit is exact there too when no other
density reads the values and no other random effect is integrated.

The term has no parameter, and the mean of the response is \code{mu}.
\code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} and \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} take \code{newdata} with one row
per row of \code{M}, read in their order, as brms takes it, so a
counterfactual such as \code{x + 1} everywhere works; when the fit dropped
rows for missing values, \code{newdata} covers them too, and a row with a
missing predictor gives \code{NA}. Other \code{newdata} is refused; brms
refuses it too unless a matrix over the new rows comes in its
\code{newdata2}. \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} gives \code{mu} on any new data. Pearson
residuals divide by the marginal sd, \eqn{\sigma \sqrt{M_{ii}}}. \code{\link[=autocor_matrix]{autocor_matrix()}} returns \code{M} over
the fitted rows.
}

\section{Families}{

This section and the two after it describe the covariance form on
\code{gaussian()} and \code{student()}, the two families with a residual
\code{y - mu}, and exactly the two brms treats this way. \code{student()} gets
the multivariate-t analog, with one \code{nu} per group, so a predicted
\code{nu ~ ...} is refused. Every other family has the model of "Latent
residuals".
}

\section{Time points, gaps and ragged groups}{

The lag between two rows is the distance between their positions in
the GLOBAL set of time levels, so a group missing week 3 gets
\code{cor(week2, week4) = rho^2}. That is \code{nlme}'s reading
(\code{corAR1(form = ~ week | subj)}) and what the agreement tests pin
down; brms instead indexes \code{ar()}/\code{ma()}/\code{arma()}/\code{cosy()} by
position within the group and treats a missing row as no gap (only
its \code{unstr()} carries a time index). On complete balanced groups the
two coincide. Time levels that are whole numbers but not consecutive
warn, because the lag is then not the one the labels suggest. Under
\code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and \code{arma()}, a group that skips a level other groups
have gives a message of class \code{frmtmb_autocor_gap}, since a brms fit
of the same formula counts that gap as one step. For brms's model,
use each row's position within its group, in time order, as the time
variable.

Groups of different sizes are handled by construction: the density is
evaluated one PATTERN at a time (a pattern being a distinct set of
present time levels), so a balanced design costs one Cholesky per
gradient evaluation and ragged data costs one per distinct pattern.
Duplicate \verb{(gr, time)} pairs are refused, as they are in brms.
}

\section{What cannot be combined with it}{

The likelihood is a joint density over each group, so it no longer
factorizes into per-row contributions. \code{weights()}, \code{cens()} and
\code{trunc()} on the same response are therefore refused, as are
\code{rescor = TRUE}, mixture families, \code{quadrature = TRUE},
\code{frm_simulate()} and \code{residuals(type = "osa")}. brms refuses
\code{weights()}, \code{cens()}, \code{trunc()} and \code{rescor = TRUE} too. Random
effects ARE allowed and are the point of the feature: the marginal
likelihood is a Laplace approximation over the modes with the
correlated residual density inside, which reproduces
\code{nlme::lme(random = ~ 1 | subj, correlation = corAR1())}.

Two addition terms keep the joint density, as in brms 2.23.0:
\itemize{
\item \code{se(s, sigma = TRUE)} adds \eqn{s_i^2}{s_i^2} to the diagonal of
each group's covariance, \eqn{D R D + \mathrm{diag}(s^2)}{D R D +
    diag(s^2)}, as brms's \code{normal_time_hom_se_lpdf()} and its relatives
do. \code{sigma} stays the innovation sd under \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and
\code{arma()}. Each group then has a covariance of its own, so the
density costs one factorization per group rather than one per
pattern. \code{se()} without \code{sigma = TRUE} is refused, as brms refuses
it: the covariance would be \code{diag(s^2)} alone, which the
correlation does not enter. \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} and \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} add the
known error to each draw, and the pointwise log-density reads the
summed covariance.
\item \code{mi()} on the response makes a missing value a coordinate of its
group's draw. A missing value that no other density reads
integrates out exactly: the density of the observed rows is the
margin of the joint one, the same block over the time points the
group still has, on \code{student()} as on \code{gaussian()}. One that another
density reads (\code{mi(y)} as a predictor) stays latent, under the
Laplace approximation, which is exact on \code{gaussian()}.
\code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} samples every missing value, as brms
does.
}
}

\section{Latent residuals}{

On a family with no residual \code{y - mu}, these terms add a latent residual
to the linear predictor of \code{mu}, one per row, as brms 2.23.0 does:
\deqn{\eta_i = \dots + err_i.}
\code{sderr} is the standard deviation of the latent residuals, with brms's
prior class \code{"sderr"}. The terms give \code{err} its law:
\describe{
\item{\code{ar()}, \code{ma()}, \code{arma()} with \code{cov = TRUE}}{Order one only. Each
group is one draw of the stationary process, and \code{sderr} is the sd of
its innovations, as \code{sigma} is on \code{gaussian()}. brms refuses a higher
order, and so does \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}}.}
\item{\code{ar()} with \code{cov = FALSE}}{Any order. The latents \code{z} are
independent, and \eqn{err_i = sderr (z_i + \sum_j ar_j z_{i-j})}{err_i
    = sderr (z_i + sum_j ar_j z_(i-j))}. Each \code{ar[j]} is in (-1, 1). This is
a moving average of the latents, by brms's own program. On a family
whose density brms writes on the response scale (\code{Gamma()}, \code{Beta()}, a
non-canonical link, a \code{cens()} or \code{trunc()} response), brms adds the sum
to \code{mu} after the inverse link, and only \code{frm_sample()} fits it; see
"Where the Laplace approximation fails". \code{ma()} and \code{arma()} are refused,
in brms's words, because AR and MA structures cannot be distinguished on
one vector of latents.}
\item{\code{cosy()}, \code{unstr()}}{\code{sderr} is the marginal sd. \code{cosy} is in
(0, 1), as brms bounds it. \code{unstr()} reads the time levels of each
row.}
}
\code{fcor()} and \code{sar()} are refused, in brms's words, and so are the
multi-category families and the mixtures, where the term would not sit on
\code{mu}, and a nonlinear \code{mu}. A nonlinear \code{shape} or \code{zi} beside a linear \code{mu}
fits, as brms builds it. The lag counts rows of a group, as in brms, and not
time levels as under the covariance form on \code{gaussian()}. If every group has
one row, \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}} refuses the term: brms builds it, but the correlation is not
seen in the data, so its chains walk a ridge.

The latents are integrated by the Laplace approximation, on sparse
coordinates that leave the approximation unchanged, so a long series costs
a banded inner Hessian and not a dense one. They are not random effects
of a group: \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}} and \code{\link[=VarCorr]{VarCorr()}} do not list them, and \code{re_formula = NA} keeps them, as brms keeps \code{err}. \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}} and \code{\link[=confint_varcorr]{confint_varcorr()}}
report \code{sderr} and the coefficients, and \code{\link[=variables]{variables()}} names them as brms
does. Under \code{frm_sample()} the draws of the latents are named
\code{latent_state[k]}. These are the chain's own states and not \code{err}: \code{ma()},
\code{arma()} and \code{cosy()} have one extra state per group, so 60 rows in 12 groups
have 72 states.

After the fit, \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}}, \code{\link[=residuals]{residuals()}}, \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} and
\code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} read the conditional mode of \code{err} on the fitted rows. On new
rows, under \code{cov = TRUE}:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} and \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} draw new latents per group of the new
data, as brms does, at the estimates of \code{sderr} and the coefficients.
\item \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} is the mean over the latent, as brms's
\code{posterior_epred()} is, by Gauss-Hermite quadrature at each row's
marginal sd. With a log link and a marginal sd of 1.15 this is 1.95 times
the value at \code{err = 0}. \code{marginaleffects} reads the same mean. Its delta
method moves \code{beta} and holds \code{sderr} and the coefficients at their
estimates, so its standard error leaves the latent's draw out, as on
any fit with a group-level term: \code{predictions()} conditions on what
\code{get_coef()} does not hold. \code{Est.Error} of \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} holds the draw
beside the coefficients', as on a new \code{me()} row, and its \code{Q} columns
read that draw: on poisson \code{Est.Error} 0.133 became the draws' 1.005.
\item \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} stays at \code{err = 0}, because it is the predictor.
}
Under \code{cov = FALSE} new rows are refused, in brms's words.
\code{\link[=conditional_effects]{conditional_effects()}} and \code{emmeans()} drop the term, as brms's do.

The joint covariance of the estimates and the latents is held as the sparse
Cholesky factor of their joint precision, which is banded along a chain, so
a standard error reads it at a cost that grows with the rows, not with
their square. \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, \code{\link[=residuals]{residuals()}} and \code{frm_linpred(se.fit = TRUE)} on
the fitted rows read each row's variance through the selected inverse of
the factor: on one poisson series, 0.4 s at 8000 rows and 1.7 s at 32000.
A hurdle or zero-inflated mean reads the latent of \code{mu} and a level of
\code{hu ~ (1 | id)} or \code{zi ~ (1 | id)} on one row, a pair that no row's
likelihood couples; that pair is read from a column of the inverse solved
once per level, 3.3 s at 32000 rows. Rows in \code{newdata} read the estimates
and the levels of any other group-level block alone, \code{\link[=frm_curve]{frm_curve()}} and
\code{\link[=conditional_effects]{conditional_effects()}} too.

A dense block of the joint covariance is formed only where a function
returns one or must form one: \code{\link[=frm_joint_cov]{frm_joint_cov()}}, \code{\link[=frm_lp_basis]{frm_lp_basis()}} on the
fitted rows, and the category probabilities of an ordinal family's fitted
rows. A block of more than \code{getOption("frmtmb.latres_dense_rows", 20000L)^2}
numbers is refused, with its size in the message. The same option bounds a
wider block: on any fit with group-level effects, latent or not, a \verb{vcov =}
that marginaleffects carries into those effects inverts the inner block, one
row per effect, densely, and past that many inner rows the effects are held
at their estimates with a warning (\link{frmtmb-marginaleffects}). Above 3000
rows the category probabilities' standard errors are \code{NA}, with a message of
class \code{frmtmb_latres_se_skipped}; \code{options(frmtmb.latres_se_rows = )} moves
that limit. A fit whose covariance of the estimates is held as a factor,
which an ill-conditioned design gives, keeps the dense joint covariance, and
both limits apply to every standard error of its fitted rows.

A coefficient near an end of its range, or an \code{sderr} near 0 against the
response's scale, is at a limit only if the fit's numbers say that end is not
determined: the standard error of log \code{sderr} is above 1, or the row's own is
not finite. \code{ar[1]} at 0.9992 on 2000 rows has a small standard error, and
reports it. At a limit the fit says so with a message of class
\code{frmtmb_boundary_fit}, \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}} gives \code{NA} for \code{Est.Error} and the
interval, and \code{\link[=diagnose]{diagnose()}} lists the limit. At \code{ar[1] = 1} with \code{sderr = 0}
the data read as \code{cosy()} or \code{(1 | group)}, and only the marginal sd is
known. At \code{ar[1] = -1} they read as one size per group whose sign alternates
by row, which \code{(1 | group)} does not fit. When \code{ar[1]} and \code{ma[1]} cancel,
the data show no correlation and neither coefficient is known.

One decision that differs from brms 2.23.0, under \code{cov = FALSE} only: brms's
\code{posterior_epred()}, \code{log_lik()} and \code{predict()} read \code{err} and leave out the
AR sum of its own Stan program, so their row densities do not add up to the
model. Summed over the rows, brms's \code{log_lik()} misses the program's
\code{log_prob} by -6.78 nats on poisson and -113.48 on Gamma(log). The methods
here read the model, whose row densities add up to \code{log_prob}. At \code{ar} 0.6
and \code{sderr} 0.5, the two means differ by up to a factor of 1.62 on poisson
and 5.18 on Gamma(log).
}

\section{Where the Laplace approximation fails}{

The inner problem of a latent residual reads what one row tells its latent.
The share of a row is \eqn{d v / (1 + d v)}{d v / (1 + d v)}, with \code{d} the
curvature of the row's log density in its linear predictor and \code{v} the
latent's variance. It has no units. Where it is small, or where a dispersion
trades off against the latent, the approximation is biased. Median over 40
data sets, against the exact maximum likelihood estimate by forward
recursion:\tabular{llll}{
   Design \tab Share \tab \code{sderr} \tab Exact \cr
   bernoulli, 20 groups of 10 \tab 0.11 \tab 0.46 \tab 1.71 \cr
