\section{Where the parameters appear}{

On the internal (unconstrained) scale they are the \verb{thetaac_*} rows
of \code{\link[=confint.frmtmb_fit]{confint.frmtmb_fit()}} and of \code{vcov(full = TRUE)}. On the natural
scale, \code{summary()} prints them under "Within-group residual
correlation" and \code{\link[=confint_varcorr]{confint_varcorr()}} reports one row per parameter
under brms's names - \code{ar[1]}, \code{ma[1]}, \code{cosy},
\verb{cortime__<t1>__<t2>} - with a delta-method interval (Fisher-z for
the bounded ones, the identity for the coefficients of a
higher-order AR/MA process). \code{\link[=hypothesis]{hypothesis()}} sees them under the
same names, \code{ar[1]} included, which brms's renaming lets an
expression carry. \code{\link[=autocor_matrix]{autocor_matrix()}} returns
the fitted \code{R}. They are NOT part of \code{\link[=VarCorr]{VarCorr()}}, which reports
random-effect blocks. \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}} targets them by brms's classes,
\code{"ar"}, \code{"ma"}, \code{"cosy"} and \code{"cortime"}, one prior slot per
parameter as in brms; see its section Residual correlation.
}

\section{The scale of \code{sigma}}{

Under the covariance form of \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and \code{arma()}, \code{sigma} is
the standard deviation of the innovations, as in brms 2.23.0, whose
\code{cholesky_cor_ar1()}, \code{cholesky_cor_ma1()} and \code{cholesky_cor_arma1()}
are the autocovariance of the process at unit innovation variance.
The marginal residual standard deviation is \code{sigma} times
\eqn{1 / \sqrt{1 - \phi^2}}{1 / sqrt(1 - phi^2)} for an AR(1),
\eqn{\sqrt{1 + \theta^2}}{sqrt(1 + theta^2)} for an MA(1), and
\eqn{\sqrt{(1 + \theta^2 + 2\phi\theta) / (1 - \phi^2)}}{sqrt((1 +
theta^2 + 2 phi theta) / (1 - phi^2))} for an ARMA(1, 1); at a higher
order the factor is the square root of the process variance at unit
innovation variance. So \code{\link[=sigma]{sigma()}}, \code{\link[=VarCorr]{VarCorr()}}, \code{summary()}, the
standard errors, \code{frm_linpred(dpar = "sigma")}, a prior on class
\code{"sigma"} and the coefficients of a \code{sigma ~ x} model are about the
innovation sd, as brms's are, and pearson residuals, \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}}
and the pointwise log-density read the marginal one. Under \code{sigma ~ x} the marginal sd of row \eqn{i} is \eqn{\sigma_i} times the same
factor, as in brms's \code{normal_time_het_lpdf()}. Under \code{cosy()},
\code{unstr()} and the \code{cov = FALSE} form, \code{sigma} is what it is in brms
too: the marginal sd, and the sd of a row around its one-step mean.

A fit saved by frmtmb 0.76.0 or earlier with one of these terms held
the marginal sd as \code{sigma}. A method whose answer reads \code{sigma} or
its coefficients refuses it; one that reads only the mean, such as
\code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} or \code{\link[=logLik]{logLik()}}, answers, since the model is the same.
\code{\link[=update]{update()}} fits it again.

\code{\link[=conditional_effects]{conditional_effects()}} with \code{method = "posterior_predict"} draws its
band at \code{sigma}, so here at the innovation sd, as brms draws it:
brms's \code{conditional_effects()} drops every autocorrelation term.
}

\section{Divergence from brms}{

brms limits \code{cov = TRUE} to order one ("Covariance formulation of
ARMA structures is only possible for effects of maximal order one");
higher \code{p} and \code{q} are supported here, with \code{sigma} the innovation sd
there too.

On a nonlinear body with \code{cov = FALSE}, brms 2.23.0's post-processing does
not follow its own model. Its Stan program carries the ARMA term in \code{mu},
but \code{predictor.bprepnl()} has no \code{predictor_ac()}, so its \code{posterior_epred()}
is the body alone and its \code{log_lik()} is the density at an ARMA coefficient
of 0: on one model with a real AR part, -185.87 against -150.99 from its own
\code{log_prob()}. \code{fitted()}, \code{predict()} and \code{logLik()} here follow the model,
as they do for a linear \code{mu}, where brms and frmtmb agree.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
d <- expand.grid(week = 1:5, subj = factor(1:30))
d$x <- rnorm(150)
e <- as.vector(vapply(1:30, function(i) {
  as.vector(stats::filter(rnorm(5), 0.6, "recursive"))
}, numeric(5)))
d$y <- 1 + 0.5 * d$x + e

fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + ar(week, subj, cov = TRUE)) + gaussian(),
           data = d)
summary(fit)
autocor_matrix(fit)

# brms's default residual-regression form: mu gains ar * (y - mu) of
# the previous row of the same subject
fit0 <- frm(bf(y ~ x + ar(week, subj)) + gaussian(), data = d)
summary(fit0)
head(fitted(fit0))

# compound symmetry, and the unstructured correlation over the five
# weeks
frm(bf(y ~ x + cosy(week, subj)) + gaussian(), data = d)
frm(bf(y ~ x + unstr(week, subj)) + gaussian(), data = d)

# a random intercept alongside the correlated residual is allowed
frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | subj) + ar(week, subj, cov = TRUE)) + gaussian(),
    data = d)

# a family with no residual: brms's latent residual, with its SD sderr
d$cnt <- rpois(150, exp(0.5 + 0.3 * d$x + e))
summary(frm(bf(cnt ~ x + ar(week, subj, cov = TRUE)), family = poisson(),
            data = d))
}
